{
  "note": "The twelve tribal allotments of Joshua 13-19, as coarse teaching outlines rather than surveyed boundaries — the same caveat atlas-polities.json carries, and more so here: Joshua's border lists name landmarks and towns, many unidentified, not continuous lines, and study maps differ from one another accordingly. Manasseh appears as two features because the tribe was split by the Jordan (Josh 13:29-31, 17:7-11); both halves share one colour. LEVI IS DELIBERATELY ABSENT: the priestly tribe received no territorial inheritance, only cities within the others (Josh 13:14, 33), so drawing it would teach the opposite of what the text says. Dan is its original western allotment (Josh 19:40-48), before the migration north to Laish in Judges 18. Dated from the atlas's own 'Dividing the land' event to the fall of Samaria, after which the northern tribal map ceased to describe anything on the ground.",
